      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\le n \\le 7 \\) and \\( n \\equiv -3737 \\pmod{8} \\), we need to determine the remainder when \\(-3737\\) is divided by \\(8\\). This can be done by finding the equivalent positive remainder within the range \\(0\\) to \\(7\\).\n\nFirst, we note that \\(-3737\\) can be expressed as:\n\\[\n-3737 = -467 \\cdot 8 + r\n\\]\nwhere \\(r\\) is the remainder when \\(-3737\\) is divided by \\(8\\). To find \\(r\\), we can use the property of modular arithmetic that states:\n\\[\n-3737 \\equiv r \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nor equivalently,\n\\[\n-3737 \\equiv 0 - (467 \\cdot 8 + r) \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[\n-3737 \\equiv - (467 \\cdot 8 + r) \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nSince \\(- (467 \\cdot 8 + r) \\equiv -467 \\cdot 8 - r \\pmod{8}\\), and noting that \\(-467 \\cdot 8\\) is a multiple of \\(8\\), we have:\n\\[\n- (467 \\cdot 8 + r) \\equiv 0 - r \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n-3737 \\equiv -r \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nr \\equiv 3737 \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\(3737\\) is divided by \\(8\\). We do this by performing the division:\n\\[\n3737 \\div 8 = 467 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n3737 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n-3737 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nSince \\(-1\\) is equivalent to \\(7\\) modulo \\(8\\) (because \\(-1 + 8 = 7\\)), we have:\n\\[\n-3737 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{8}\n\\]\nThus, the integer \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]",
