      "text": "To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\le n \\le 7 \\) and \\( n \\equiv -3737 \\pmod{8} \\), we need to determine the remainder when \\(-3737\\) is divided by \\(8\\). This can be done using the property of congruences that relates the negative to the positive remainder.\n\nFirst, we find the remainder when \\(3737\\) is divided by \\(8\\). We do this by performing the division:\n\n\\[ 3737 \\div 8 = 467 \\text{ remainder } 1. \\]\n\nTo verify, we can calculate:\n\n\\[ 3737 = 8 \\cdot 467 + 1. \\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\(3737\\) is divided by \\(8\\) is \\(1\\). This means:\n\n\\[ 3737 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{8}. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when \\(-3737\\) is divided by \\(8\\). Since \\(-3737 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{8}\\) (because \\(-3737 + 3739 = 2\\), and \\(3739 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{8}\\)), we can write:\n\n\\[ -3737 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{8}. \\]\n\nThis can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ -1 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{8}. \\]\n\nThis is because \\(-1\\) is equivalent to \\(7\\) in modulo \\(8\\) arithmetic because \\(-1 + 8 = 7\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{8}. \\]\n\nSince \\(0 \\le n \\le 7\\), the integer \\(n\\) that satisfies this congruence is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{7}. \\]",
